---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import DBox from '../../components/DBox.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '');
---

<Base
  title="voice studio — live demo — Andrew Yang"
  description="A paid, long-running job split across two runtimes: a producer in Kubernetes, a worker on the host, a queue that carries only an id, and a contract that cannot drift silently."
>
  <p><a href={`${base}/demo/`}>← Demos</a></p>
  <span class="tag">queues · two runtimes</span>
  <h1>voice studio: a job that costs money, crossing a runtime boundary</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Paste a video URL and a script; get back an mp3 of that voice reading it.
    The product is four minutes of waiting, which makes this an exercise in the
    unglamorous half of distributed systems: where the state lives, what the
    queue is allowed to carry, and how two services written in two languages
    stay in agreement.
  </p>

  <Diagram
    viewBox="0 0 920 314"
    alt="A static page posts a job to a producer running in Kubernetes, which writes a database row before publishing a message containing only the job id. A worker running in host Docker consumes it, downloads the audio, cuts a sample, clones the voice and synthesises the script, writing status and the finished file straight back to the shared PostgreSQL. The browser polls the backend, which reads the same rows. A failed job retries once and then stops in a dead-letter queue."
    caption="Two runtimes, one shared database. The queue carries an identifier and nothing else — every other arrow in the picture is state, not messaging."
  >
    <DBox x={10} y={24} title="Static page" sub="login, a URL and a script" />
    <DBox x={335} y={24} title="Producer (Kubernetes)" sub="row first, then publish" sub2="— a ghost job is the worse failure" />
    <DBox x={660} y={24} title="Job queue" sub="one field: the job id" />

    <DBox x={660} y={134} title="Worker (host Docker)" sub="download → sample → clone" sub2="→ speak → delete the model" />
    <DBox x={335} y={134} accent title="PostgreSQL" sub="both runtimes share only this:" sub2="status per stage, and the file" />
    <DBox x={10} y={134} title="Browser polls the API" sub="one stage name at a time" />

    <DBox x={10} y={244} title="Download the result" sub="when, and only when, done" />
    <DBox x={660} y={244} dashed title="One retry, then stop" sub="dead letter — the job is already" sub2="marked failed where you can see it" />

    <path class="flow" d="M240,54 L329,54" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow" d="M565,54 L654,54" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow" d="M775,84 L775,128" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow" d="M660,164 L571,164" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow" d="M335,164 L246,164" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow" d="M125,194 L125,238" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow dash" d="M775,194 L775,238" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
  </Diagram>

  <h2>What each reader tends to ask</h2>
  <div class="lenses">
    <div class="lens">
      <span class="tag">business</span>
      <h3>Every request spends someone's money</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Nothing runs for an anonymous visitor.</strong> The whole
          feature sits behind a login, because the cheapest abuse of a paid
          pipeline is the one that costs the attacker nothing.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Limits at both ends.</strong> The script length and the URL
          shape are checked before the job is accepted; the video length and the
          output size are checked again by the worker, before the expensive
          steps. A limit only enforced at the front door is a suggestion.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Failure is bounded, not retried into a bill.</strong> A deleted
          video fails identically every time — so it retries once, then stops
          somewhere visible instead of burning quota in a loop.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Nothing is left behind at the vendor.</strong> The voice model
          is deleted as soon as the audio is rendered; the account does not
          accumulate orphans nobody is auditing.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="lens">
      <span class="tag">engineering</span>
      <h3>The contract is the interesting part</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The queue is not a data channel.</strong> The message carries
          an id; the worker reads the payload from the database. Messages get
          replayed, and a replayed message that carries a stale copy of the
          truth is a bug you find months later.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write the row, then publish.</strong> If the publish fails the
          row is marked failed. A job that exists in the queue but not in the
          database is invisible; a job that exists in the database and failed is
          a support ticket you can actually answer.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A contract that breaks loudly.</strong> Both sides — Kotlin and
          Python — declare the same queue topology, and the broker refuses a
          redeclaration whose arguments disagree. Two copies of a contract will
          drift; the design decision is to make the drift fail on the first
          connection rather than on the first failure.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Progress without a second queue.</strong> Each stage updates a
          status column and the browser polls. For one user watching one job, a
          reply queue would be more machinery and less observability.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="lens">
      <span class="tag">AI</span>
      <h3>The model is a paid dependency, so it is held at arm's length</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The provider is an interface.</strong> Cloning and synthesis
          sit behind one small port with a stub implementation, so the entire
          pipeline — queue, retries, database, download — is testable end to end
          without spending anything or being online.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The expensive step is opt-in.</strong> Vocal separation is a
          switch that is off by default: I would rather turn on a cost when a
          sample needs it than pay it on every job by habit.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Deterministic failures do not deserve retries.</strong> This
          pipeline's errors are mostly not stochastic — an unavailable video, a
          script over quota. Retrying those is not resilience, it is repetition.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Consent is a design constraint, not a footnote.</strong> Every
          vendor's terms require the voice owner's permission, which is why this
          is a tool for cloning my own voice and the login is not decoration.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>What I would rather show you in person</strong></p>
    <ul class="clean">
      <li>The three-row contract table between the two repositories — and what happens on the connection after you change one side of it.</li>
      <li>The credential bug that shipped green: the integration test connected with the broker's default account, so a producer that ignored its configured password passed every test.</li>
      <li>A password that lost a byte in transit between two encrypted stores, and why the fix was to make every path agree on the trailing whitespace rather than to re-type it.</li>
      <li>Why the audio lives in the database today, and the size at which that becomes the wrong answer.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>See it</h2>
  <p>
    This is the one demo you cannot open yourself: every job spends real money
    at a vendor, so there is no anonymous entry point — an honest constraint is
    better than a public form I would have to police. What is open:<br />
    <a href="https://github.com/nplus-father/voice-worker">the worker</a>
     — pipeline, retry semantics and the contract notes.<br />
    <a href="https://github.com/nplus-father/voice-studio-site">the entry page</a>
     — a static page with no build step, on purpose.
  </p>
</Base>
